    "case_id": "103",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 20s with complex congenital heart disease that included D-transposition of the great arteries, single ventricle physiology (upstairs-downstairs configuration of the ventricles with a large ventricular septal defect with outflow extension), and Fontan circulation was admitted with lightheadedness and syncope that occurred while walking. He had previously been prescribed diltiazem 120 mg daily and apixaban 5 mg twice daily after an initial episode. He returned a few days later with persistent tachycardia and palpitations.\n\nHeart rate of 183 beats per minute (bpm) in the emergency department.\n\n- Electrocardiogram (ECG):  Image Title: Figure 1A and 1B - Electrocardiograms of a Narrow Complex Tachycardia  Image Description: Shows irregularly irregular tachycardia with grouped beating and intermittently visible P waves in lead V1. Rhythm strip shows 3 consistent beats grouped together with 560-millisecond duration.- Transesophageal echocardiogram:  Finding: No intracardiac thrombus detected.- Additional ECG after diltiazem:  Image Title: Figure 2A - ECG after diltiazem administration  Image Description: Regular narrow complex tachycardia with visible P waves in lead II, cycle length between visible P waves is 440 milliseconds.- ECG with adenosine:  Image Title: Figure 2B - ECG after adenosine administration  Image Description: Shows temporary heart block with continuation of supraventricular tachycardia with cycle length of 220 ms.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Ectopic Atrial Tachycardia\nB. Intra-atrial reentrant tachycardia (IART) with variable atrioventricular block\nC. Atrial Tachycardia with variable block\nD. Atrial Flutter with variable conduction\n",
